# XCU 1.1 Relationship to Other Documents
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The defaults of XCU 1.1 (process attributes, file creation, file removal,
# pathname resolution, arithmetic) are observed through standard utilities.
# Operators and mathematical functions of the languages are tested where the
# languages are (XCU.2.6-word-expansions, XCU.awk).

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.1/Process Attributes
class      required
since      1992
says       A process created by a utility's fork-and-exec has the attributes of
           the creating process that the standard lists: current directory, file
           mode creation mask, open file descriptors, real and effective user and
           group IDs, supplementary groups and process group.
test       t/001-process-attributes.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.2/Independent processes
class      required
since      1992
says       A process started by another keeps running when its creator exits: a
           background subshell started by a shell that then exits still completes
           its work.
timeout    60
test       t/002-independent-processes.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/user ID of the file
class      required
since      1992
says       A file that does not exist and is created by a utility or a shell
           redirection is a regular file of length zero owned by the effective
           user, with group the effective group or the directory's group, and
           mode 0666 less the umask; a created directory is empty with mode 0777
           less the umask, a created FIFO has mode 0666 less the umask, and the
           file times are current.
timeout    60
test       t/003-creation-defaults.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/shall be truncated to zero length
class      required
since      1992
says       When a utility creates a regular file that already exists as a regular
           file, the file is truncated to zero length, its owner, group and
           permission bits do not change, and its modification time is updated;
           tee, a shell redirection, sort -o, dd of=, sed w and an awk
           redirection all behave so.
test       t/004-create-over-regular.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/Fail.
class      required
since      1992
says       Creating a file where one of an incompatible type exists fails with a
           diagnostic and leaves the existing object as it was: tee onto a
           directory; mkdir over a directory, file or symbolic link; mkfifo over
           a file, FIFO, directory or symbolic link; ln -s over a file, symbolic
           link or FIFO.
test       t/005-create-fails-existing.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/Follow link
class      required
since      1992
says       When a utility creates a regular file and the name is an existing
           symbolic link, the link is followed: the target is written, and a
           missing target is created; the link itself stays a link.
test       t/006-create-follows-symlink.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/Open FIFO
class      required
since      1992
says       When a utility writes a regular file whose name is an existing FIFO,
           it waits until the FIFO is open for reading and then opens it for
           writing and carries on, so the data reaches the reader.
timeout    60
test       t/007-create-over-fifo.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/O_APPEND
class      required
since      1992
says       When a utility appends to a file it opens it for appending without
           truncating: tee -a keeps the old contents, creates a missing file, and
           writes at the current end of a file that another process has meanwhile
           emptied.
timeout    60
test       t/008-append-mode.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.4/access mode corresponding to the operation
class      required
since      1992
says       A utility opens a file to read it with read access only and to write
           it with write access only: a file the user can only read can be read
           by cat, sort, wc, head, cksum, od, grep, sed, cut and awk, and a file
           the user can only write can be written by tee, tee -a, a redirection,
           dd, sort -o, cp and sed w.
test       t/009-access-modes.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.010
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.5/link count
class      required
since      1992
says       Removing a file takes its name away and decrements its link count; the
           contents of a removed file stay readable through a descriptor opened
           earlier; rmdir removes an empty directory; and removing a name from a
           directory updates that directory's modification time.
timeout    60
test       t/010-file-removal.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.011
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.1.8/Pathname Resolution
class      required
since      1992
says       Utilities resolve pathnames as the file hierarchy defines: dot, dot-
           dot, repeated slashes and symbolic link components work, a trailing
           slash after a non-directory and an empty pathname are errors.
test       t/011-pathname-resolution.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.012
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1/Arithmetic Precision and Operations
class      required
since      1992
says       Integer values in the shell's arithmetic, in expr and in test cover at
           least the range of a signed C long of 32 bits: 2147483647 and
           -2147483648 can be produced, printed and compared.
test       t/012-integer-long-range.sh

assertion  XCU.1.1-relationship.013
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1/initialized to zero
class      required
since      1992
says       Variables of the standard utilities' own languages start at zero when
           the input does not assign them: an awk variable or array element, a bc
           variable or array element, and an unset shell name in arithmetic.
test       t/013-uninitialized-zero.sh
